# Supply-chain cooldown — the package-manager half of ADR-0027's release-age
# layer. npm resolves only versions published more than this many days ago.
#
# Renovate applies its own 3-day cooldown to npm updates (config:best-practices
# extends security:minimumReleaseAgeNpm), but it CANNOT age the paths where the
# package manager does the resolving — `lockFileMaintenance` is marked
# unsupported for exactly that reason. That path runs daily here, is exempt from
# the PR limits, and auto-merges, so it is the one that needs npm-side cover.
# Renovate detects this key and defers to npm rather than passing --before.
#
# Keep the value at 3 to match the Renovate preset. Larger values only widen the
# window below. `npm ci` replays the lockfile and is unaffected.
#
# ETARGET, and why nothing recovers from it. npm flattens this key to
# `before = now - 3 days` for EVERY resolution — there is no security-update
# exemption, unlike Renovate's own half. A direct range whose floor is newer than
# the cutoff fails:
#
#   npm error code ETARGET
#   npm error notarget No matching version found for X with a date before <date>.
#
# Renovate retries lockfile generation without --before on ETARGET, but the retry
# is guarded on that flag being set and Renovate leaves it empty precisely
# because this key is here. So a CVE fix published today lands as a broken PR
# until someone runs `npm install --min-release-age=0` (--before is refused as
# mutually exclusive). Accepted tradeoff — see docs/ops/dep-management.md
# § When resolution ETARGETs and ADR-0027 § 2026-08-28 amendment.
min-release-age=3
